<p class="lead">
  A scanned document that came out of order, a report where the appendix should move before the conclusion, a PDF built from files added in the wrong sequence — reordering pages by hand usually means splitting the file apart and merging it back together in the right order. There's a more direct way.
</p>

<h2>Reorder pages in your browser (no upload, free)</h2>
<p>
  The <a href="/tools/reorder-pdf/">Reorder PDF Pages tool</a> on keptlocal renders a thumbnail of every page and lets you drag them into the order you want, entirely on your device.
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Drop your PDF onto the tool. Thumbnails of every page load into a grid.</li>
  <li>Drag pages into the order you want.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Download</strong> — the reordered PDF saves immediately.</li>
</ol>
<p>
  Because you're dragging visual thumbnails rather than typing page numbers, it's much harder to make an off-by-one mistake than with a numeric "new order" field.
</p>

<h2>Why this beats split-and-remerge</h2>
<p>
  Before a visual reorder tool, fixing page order meant three separate steps: split the PDF into individual pages, note which file corresponds to which position, then merge them back together in the corrected sequence — a process with several opportunities to mislabel a file or merge in the wrong order, especially past a handful of pages. Dragging thumbnails directly into place removes the indirection: what you see is the order you get.
</p>

<h2>Common reasons pages end up out of order</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Scanning</strong> — a double-sided document scanned single-sided produces odd and even pages as two separate passes, which then need interleaving; a stack fed into an auto-feeder slightly out of order comes out slightly out of order.</li>
  <li><strong>Document assembly</strong> — combining sections from different sources (an appendix added after the fact, a revised chapter inserted) often lands pages at the end rather than where they belong.</li>
  <li><strong>Export order</strong> — some software exports pages in the order they were created or edited rather than a logical reading order.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Privacy: what happens to your file</h2>
<p>
  Thumbnail rendering uses pdf.js and the actual reordering uses pdf-lib — both running locally in your browser tab. Your PDF is never uploaded. Open DevTools → Network while using the tool and you'll see no outbound requests, even while the thumbnails are being generated.
</p>

